An adversarial review of yesterday's two guards found TEN defects, two of which
inverted a guard's purpose. All ten are fixed and pinned by regression tests named
for their incidents. NO TEST WAS REPAIRED — that remains the operator's call.

=== SERIOUS 1: rule 4 refused the project's own documented substrate convention ===

adversarial_verify.py documents `<canonical value><separator><human note>` and
strips the note before matching. _strength_rank did not — it scanned the WHOLE
string including the prose and took the minimum band. 233+ live declarations use
that form.

exp5178 declares `live_llm_embedding_extraction; Substrate corrected 2026-07-03:
... verifier_ensemble_against_cached_candidates also does not fit (its definition
explicitly requires the LLM NOT be loaded, and this task did load it ...)`. The
leading token is a real GGUF load (band 3), but the word `cached` in the note
explaining why cached-candidate scoring does NOT apply dragged it to band 2 — so
the lint refused the commit while asserting the opposite of what the artifact
says. exp5161 is the same shape. Both are CLAUDE.md's own named exemplars for
their substrates.

This is the negation-blindness class CLAUDE.md's QA-Layer Authenticity Discipline
names: a checker confusing "did X" with "explicitly did NOT do X".

Fixed by delegating the match to adversarial_verify._match_declared_substrate
rather than re-deriving separator handling — a drifted copy of a matcher is
exactly how the bug arose — and by accepting a rationale carried INLINE in the
value, which is where the most carefully documented corrections put it.
Verified corpus-wide, not just on the exemplars: of all 2,673 inference_substrate
declarations in the tree, ZERO now rank differently from their bare leading token.

=== SERIOUS 2: the pre-commit interlock was disarmed by the exact invocation that caused both incidents ===

--gate refuses while ops/.test_suite_mutation_pending.json exists, and ONLY --run
ever wrote that marker. So the interlock protected the wrapper nobody uses and was
silent for `pytest tests/python/test_arc_*.py tests/python/test_experiment_*.py`.

Demonstrated on the real tree: after simulating that rewrite class, README.md
(operator-curated, one of the original 39) and openspec/papers/paper-v6/
section-6-limitations.md were both modified while --gate exited 0 AND the
determination lint printed OK.

Fixed at the source rather than the wrapper: tests/python/conftest.py takes a git
baseline in pytest_configure and calls arm_from_pytest() in pytest_sessionfinish,
so the marker arms HOWEVER pytest was invoked. Proven end-to-end — a green
single-test run that rewrites README.md now warns, arms the marker, and --gate
exits 1; tree restored and verified byte-identical. Three properties pinned because
each could quietly undo it: skips xdist WORKERS (only the controller may arm),
DISARMS on a clean run, and NEVER restores (auto-reverting has destroyed in-flight
work twice). Wiring --check into pre-commit was rejected: with no baseline it
cannot tell a test's rewrite from a human's edit and would refuse every commit on
a dirty tree.

KNOWN GAP, stated not hidden: a SIGKILL'd or timed-out run never arms, because
pytest_sessionfinish does not run. Observed directly while validating. Use --run
for long jobs.

=== THE OTHER EIGHT ===

* Phantom LIVE band from a non-substrate name. exp5240 declared
  `arc_live_path_patch_synthesis` — the ARC live CODE path, never a legal enum
  value — so a token scan read its `live` token as LIVE/HARDWARE and refused the
  honest later correction to `aggregation_from_upstream_artifacts` as a downgrade.
  Fixed structurally: for the enum-governed field an unrecognised name may rank
  WEAK but never STRONG. A claim of strength is cheap to make by accident; an
  admission of weakness (`mock`, `blocked`) is not. A first attempt returned None
  for ALL non-enum values and silently un-protected `sota_gguf_mock` — CLAUDE.md's
  own fabrication exemplar — and two PRE-EXISTING tests caught it.
* Calibration restated with its window defined. "1,200 commits" was ambiguous
  between 1,200 overall (3,232 pairs) and 1,200 touching results/ (7,024 pairs).
  Re-run over the latter after the repair: R3 10x, R4 12x. The blanket "every R4
  hit is a real incident" is replaced by per-hit adjudication. A pre-repair sweep
  counted 15 R4 hits; the three that vanished are the honest-correction class
  above, and the count going DOWN is the guard getting more correct.
* Four uncovered review-output markers added: n_samples_justification (56 — the
  sample-size disclosure CLAUDE.md REQUIRES for a distributional claim),
  false_negative_risk_checked (63), paper_v6_forbidden_claims (22),
  adversarial_verify_flags (22). honest_verdict (5,245) stays excluded and is now
  pinned by a test, since a re-run legitimately produces a new one.
* A census figure did not reproduce: the `correct`-exclusion rationale cited 601
  artifacts; the stated predicate yields 465. Design unaffected, number corrected.
* Three documentation claims overstated their evidence, corrected in place: only
  2 of 13 "unattributed — subprocess writer" rows actually contain a subprocess
  (9 have none and are re-runnable with the existing plugin); "All 20 runpy files"
  is what a LITERAL-PATH grep can see and misses at least 3 variable-target sites;
  the per-batch table stopped at batch 02 of 13 and is marked superseded by the
  whole-suite union run that actually backs the coverage claim.
* Option (E) proposed CARNOT_RESULTS_DIR, which occurs ZERO times in the tree. The
  convention it describes already exists and works: CARNOT_REPO_ROOT, in 52 scripts
  and 56 test files, and it is precisely why the 3 variable-target runpy sites
  rewrite nothing. The operator was being asked to approve a design call without
  being told a 52-script precedent for it was already in the tree.

Mutation testing, again: 10 mutations, 9 killed, 1 survivor — reverting token
anchoring to a bare substring left the suite green, so the anchoring this work
claims was entirely untested. It matters because the rank is a MINIMUM:
`uncached_live_gguf_inference` unanchored matches `cached` (band 2) and
`unblocked_live_gpu_run` matches `blocked` (band 0) — the same negation blindness
as SERIOUS 1. Pinned; final 10/10 killed, 56 tests green across both guard suites.

=== WHAT IS NOW GUARDED ===

* A commit that DROPS a correction / provenance / disclosure / review-note field
  from results/*.json is refused (rule 3, 4 new marker patterns).
* A commit that WEAKENS a substrate/mode declaration in place without a note is
  refused (rule 4) — and no longer refuses the honest-correction convention.
* A test run that rewrites tracked files arms a pre-commit interlock HOWEVER
  pytest was invoked, not only under the --run wrapper.

=== WHAT REMAINS UNFIXED ===

THE 11 TESTS. They still re-execute real experiment scripts, which still overwrite
their own committed artifacts on every suite run. The guards make that
un-committable; they do not stop it happening. Repairing the tests is an operator
design call with a visible tradeoff, and the operator is asleep.

=== THE OPERATOR'S OPTIONS (six, costed in the survey §6 — I am not choosing) ===

  (A) per-script output-dir opt-in   — REJECTED by evidence: runpy is 1 of 41; the
                                       real class is 704 files calling main().
  (B) snapshot-and-restore per call  — REJECT: the suite runs -n 4, so it is racy
                                       by construction.
  (C) assert against the committed artifact — stops exercising the script at all.
  (D) rely on the guards             — no coverage lost; detection, not prevention.
  (E) resolve output paths centrally — extend the EXISTING CARNOT_REPO_ROOT
                                       convention (52 scripts / 56 tests), do not
                                       invent a second variable.
  (F) fix the 139 hardcoded absolute paths — also a standalone G2 reproducibility
                                       defect: those scripts run on one machine.

  MY RECOMMENDATION, revised twice as evidence came in: (D) now → (F) → (E).
  Explicitly NOT (A). Reject (B). The call is yours.

Two things worth your eyes regardless: README.md is rewritten by
test_experiment_209_cleanup.py (operator-curated per Public Documentation
Discipline, and NOT among the hazard commit's 39), and
results/experiment_410_precision_live.json is written by test_experiment_431_… —
any repair that pairs test_experiment_N with experiment_N by name will miss it.

